.TH "LMM" "1" "Nov 2026" "lmm 2.3.0" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
lmm-profile-export - Export a profile as a shareable collection


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBlmm profile export <name> [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Export a profile as an lmm collection: a portable, shareable document
(format version 1, see docs/collection-format.md).

.PP
Without -o the document is written to stdout as YAML, so it can be
redirected to a file, diffed or kept in git. -o <file> writes a compressed
\&.lmmc container instead; add --plain to write the YAML to the file.

.PP
The document lists the profile's mods (with their names, so a recipient
without the source still sees what each is), its hooks and config
overrides, and the game by a portable identity (its Steam app id and its
ids on each source) beside this machine's game id. Your link method and
the default flag stay on this machine.

.PP
A collection has a stable id: the first export of a profile records one in
the profile, and every later export keeps it. --title, --author,
--description and --instructions add the words a recipient sees before
importing, and --collection-version numbers each version you publish. What
these flags set is saved in the profile, so the next export reuses it and
you only pass what changes; the version is never raised for you.

.PP
A collection points at each mod on its source; it never carries a
source's files. Files of your own - a patch, a config pack - can travel
inside the .lmmc: import them into the profile as a mod first ('lmm
import <archive>\&'), then name that mod with --bundle (as source:id, its
id, or its name; repeatable). A mod from NexusMods, CurseForge or any
other source is refused. A mod a collection already bundled is carried
again whether or not you name it. Bundled files need the .lmmc container,
so --bundle needs -o and refuses --plain.

.PP
--json prints the profile document of #309 instead, unchanged.

.PP
Examples:
  lmm profile export survival --game skyrim-se > survival.yaml
  lmm profile export survival --game skyrim-se -o survival.lmmc
  lmm profile export survival --game skyrim-se -o survival.yaml --plain
  lmm profile export survival --game skyrim-se -o survival.lmmc --author me --collection-version 2
  lmm profile export survival --game skyrim-se -o survival.lmmc --bundle "My Patch"


.SH OPTIONS
\fB--author\fP=""
	the collection's author

.PP
\fB--bundle\fP=[]
	carry this mod's files in the .lmmc as your own (repeatable; a mod imported from a file, named as source:id, its id or its name)

.PP
\fB--collection-version\fP=0
	this publish's version number (default: the profile's, else 1)

.PP
\fB--description\fP=""
	a description shown to the recipient

.PP
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for export

.PP
\fB--instructions\fP=""
	install instructions shown to the recipient

.PP
\fB-o\fP, \fB--output\fP=""
	write the collection to this file (a compressed .lmmc unless --plain)

.PP
\fB--plain\fP[=false]
	with -o, write the YAML document instead of a .lmmc container

.PP
\fB--title\fP=""
	the collection's display name


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB--config\fP=""
	config directory (default: $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/lmm or ~/.config/lmm)

.PP
\fB--data\fP=""
	data directory (default: $XDG_DATA_HOME/lmm or ~/.local/share/lmm)

.PP
\fB-g\fP, \fB--game\fP=""
	game ID to operate on

.PP
\fB--json\fP[=false]
	output JSON instead of text; mutating commands print their result, --dry-run prints the plan; never prompts

.PP
\fB--log-level\fP="off"
	diagnostic log level written to stderr (off, error, warn, info, debug)

.PP
\fB--no-color\fP[=false]
	disable colored output (NO_COLOR env is also honored)

.PP
\fB--no-hooks\fP[=false]
	disable all hooks

.PP
\fB-v\fP, \fB--verbose\fP[=false]
	verbose output


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBlmm-profile(1)\fP


